In this lesson we'll look at the basic exponential graph and why it behaves so differently from a straight line. An exponential function is one where the variable sits up in the exponent, like y equals two to the x, and the base has to be positive but not equal to one. Now here's the cool part about its shape. No matter what valid base you pick, the graph always crosses the y-axis at one, because anything to the power of zero equals one. The curve also never touches the x-axis, since raising a positive number to any power gives something greater than zero. When the base is bigger than one, the curve climbs steeply to the right, which we call exponential growth. When the base is between zero and one, the curve does the opposite and decays away to the right. So how is this different from linear? A linear rule adds the same amount each step, while an exponential rule multiplies by the same factor each step, so an exponential always catches up and overtakes a line eventually, even a tiny growth rate wins in the long run. Now let's see it in action.